{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Rectifier Activation Function.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Rectifier Activation Function.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"rectifier activation function rectifier activation function","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Rectifier Activation Function.md","fileName":"Rectifier Activation Function.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Rectifier Activation Function","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-06 00:13\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Artificial Neural Network Structure]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Artificial Neural Network Structure","Activation Function","Vanishing Gradient Problem","algorithms.epub","deeplearning_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Rectifier Activation Function.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Rectifier Activation Function.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"rectifier activation function rectifier activation function","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Rectifier Activation Function.md","fileName":"Rectifier Activation Function.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Rectifier Activation Function","heading":"Rectifier Activation Function","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The rectifier activation function maps every negative input to zero and returns positive input in direct proportion to its value. A neuron using this function is called a rectified linear unit, or ReLU.\n\nUnlike sigmoid and tanh, the positive side does not flatten toward a fixed maximum. Different neural architectures favor different [[Activation Function|activation functions]], and ReLU units are common building blocks in deep networks.\n\nKelleher emphasizes the training tradeoff: the rectifier's derivative is one on its positive side, letting gradients pass through active units without the repeated shrinkage of a saturated sigmoid. Its negative side has zero gradient, however, so inactive units do not train from that local signal. A leaky variant introduces a nonzero negative-side slope. The activation choice affects both representational behavior and the [[Vanishing Gradient Problem|flow of training gradients]].","tags":["baby"],"links":["Artificial Neural Network Structure","Activation Function","Vanishing Gradient Problem","algorithms.epub","deeplearning_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Rectifier Activation Function.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Rectifier Activation Function.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"rectifier activation function rectifier activation function","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Rectifier Activation Function.md","fileName":"Rectifier Activation Function.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Rectifier Activation Function","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[algorithms.epub]]\n\n[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Artificial Neural Network Structure","Activation Function","Vanishing Gradient Problem","algorithms.epub","deeplearning_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}